    key: 'learning-spaces', title: 'A Flexible Learning Space', level: 'B2',
    text: 'A university converted an unused administrative floor into a learning space. The old offices had fixed desks and narrow corridors, while the new design included quiet booths, shared tables, movable screens, and a small recording room. The designers did not assume that one arrangement would suit every student. They asked learners when they needed silence, when they worked with others, and which equipment was difficult to find elsewhere on campus.\n\nDuring the first semester, the space was busiest before examinations. Students appreciated the variety, but some complained that group conversations reached the quiet booths. In response, managers changed the layout, added signs, and introduced a booking period for the recording room. They also kept part of the floor unbooked so that students who had not planned ahead could still find a place to work.\n\nThe university evaluated the project through observation, short surveys, and interviews. It found that students who used the space for group projects valued movable screens, while students preparing presentations valued the recording room. However, attendance alone did not show whether students had learned more. Some learners visited briefly to print a document, and others stayed for hours without using the study resources. The evaluation team therefore described the space as supporting learning rather than proving it caused higher achievement.\n\nThe project’s most useful feature may be its willingness to change. Furniture and technology mattered, but management mattered too. A flexible space is not simply an attractive room; it is a service that responds to competing needs, explains its rules, and uses evidence without making claims that the evidence cannot support.',
